{"name":"Anchors Away","hex":"#9ebbcd","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"anchors","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of anchor"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of anchor"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/anchors"},{"term":"away","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["From a place, hence.","Aside; off; in another direction."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Away with you! Go away! Begone!"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/away"}],"entries":[{"title":"Anchors Aweigh","blocks":[{"text":"Anchors Aweigh is the fight song of the United States Naval Academy and unofficial march song of the United States Navy. It was composed in 1906 by Charles A. Zimmermann with lyrics by Alfred Hart Miles. When he composed Anchors Aweigh, Zimmermann was a lieutenant and had been bandmaster of the United States Naval Academy Band since 1887. Miles was midshipman first class at the academy, in the class of 1907, and had asked Zimmermann to assist him in composing a song for that class, to be used as a football march. Another academy midshipman, Royal Lovell, later wrote what would be adopted into the song as its third verse. Another member of the Naval Academy Band, Willy Perlitz Jr., assisted in writing the music for the different instruments used in Anchors Aweigh."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/AnchorsAweigh.jpeg/330px-AnchorsAweigh.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anchors_Aweigh"},{"title":"Anchor","blocks":[{"text":"An anchor is a device, normally made of metal, used to secure a vessel to the bed of a body of water to prevent the craft from drifting due to wind or current. The word derives from Latin ancora, which itself comes from the Greek ἄγκυρα."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Kotwica_SS_Pozna%C5%84.JPG/330px-Kotwica_SS_Pozna%C5%84.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anchor"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Anchors_Aweigh_%281907%29.pdf/page1-330px-Anchors_Aweigh_%281907%29.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Anchors_Aweigh_%281907%29.pdf/page1-330px-Anchors_Aweigh_%281907%29.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"1943 sheet music","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAnchors_Aweigh_(1907).pdf"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Amoco_Cadiz_anchor.jpg/500px-Amoco_Cadiz_anchor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Amoco_Cadiz_anchor.jpg/1280px-Amoco_Cadiz_anchor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Amoco_Cadiz_anchor.jpg/500px-Amoco_Cadiz_anchor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Anchor of Amoco Cadiz in Portsall, north-west Brittany, France","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmoco_Cadiz_anchor.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Ankkuri_Kirjurin_k%C3%A4rjess%C3%A4_1.jpg/500px-Ankkuri_Kirjurin_k%C3%A4rjess%C3%A4_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Ankkuri_Kirjurin_k%C3%A4rjess%C3%A4_1.jpg/1280px-Ankkuri_Kirjurin_k%C3%A4rjess%C3%A4_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Ankkuri_Kirjurin_k%C3%A4rjess%C3%A4_1.jpg/500px-Ankkuri_Kirjurin_k%C3%A4rjess%C